#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dfb93ac-6115-54ec-b40b-3b469b909b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321375} {:answer/id #uuid "2a46af78-157d-5308-b26d-327443fe498e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321472} {:answer/id #uuid "55911fc0-26aa-5938-a1d7-93d966b3956b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321825} {:answer/id #uuid "a9beb07d-cec7-52a1-8551-72c150518a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322480} {:answer/id #uuid "b31b3202-b319-59da-91e9-f4fb84c180cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b31b3202-b319-59da-91e9-f4fb84c180cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322546} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e128d82-9568-5a46-812a-6563bf8f3dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bed1a457-fd38-5a3f-94a3-0c655ad8bedf" :question/math-academy-id "q-38001" :question/problem "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{11}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n-11{x}_{2}+11{x}_{3}=11\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}-5(-1+{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=9\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2-{x}_{3} \\\\ -1+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2-1=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124520 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c88b4ee6-35df-5639-9778-2c48196a292b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123297} {:answer/id #uuid "d26e5777-7891-5d5f-892a-2c850b9c1f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123298} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb2bf53-07a3-5edf-9116-1866c25ac0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123299} {:answer/id #uuid "0b063e06-3252-5f4a-96d3-be2f93ac711b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123300} {:answer/id #uuid "4184f8cf-f7e2-5981-9b6f-1766bc13db7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4184f8cf-f7e2-5981-9b6f-1766bc13db7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123301} :answer-field/id #uuid "e64b6253-fb87-5051-b467-09c16a35ffa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "994a2790-60b0-54d4-bc90-ddef3472f156" :question/math-academy-id "q-38005" :question/problem "If $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $1$ pivot column, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "162ce159-8146-5343-b673-2bb04870d49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421432} {:answer/id #uuid "33a2a109-2527-55af-bbae-ca17356c27f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421434} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8760df-a057-50f7-9fb8-ea0e266e838b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421435} {:answer/id #uuid "d52e325b-57b3-5106-9fdd-f84a1cb74aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421444} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c73eea-57b4-5135-a689-e2351a61cbfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "162ce159-8146-5343-b673-2bb04870d49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421432} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7a56c57-7ab7-579a-a5d2-335e803bfe6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "59614e83-6cc0-5ed3-9c08-72ebbf1a6792" :question/math-academy-id "q-38011" :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{4},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{4} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4} \\\\ 7{x}_{4}-4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -3 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 10 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -1 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 10 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -16 & 32 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has three pivot columns (the $1$st, $2$nd and the $4$th). Hence ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the third equation we get \n\n$$\n-16{x}_{4}=32\\,⟹\\,{x}_{4}=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}+10(-2)=-10\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=5+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting ${x}_{2}=5+{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}=2$ into the first equation we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(5+{x}_{3})-2(-2)=5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-4-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4-{x}_{3} \\\\ 5+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=0$, we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{4}=-4+5-2=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff34fdc-0a64-5ac7-9e6c-4593625c616c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421431} {:answer/id #uuid "44c429a9-15eb-57f2-b546-ca949bfb2581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421436} {:answer/id #uuid "c861a1e5-85f4-5f47-a6c6-f3a0e912537f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421443} {:answer/id #uuid "f25a09b3-86fe-5afc-8268-40b897c03f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421449} {:answer/id #uuid "f96f6aac-f80f-53db-9b40-142a8715dce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f25a09b3-86fe-5afc-8268-40b897c03f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421449} :answer-field/id #uuid "971b0f6e-9510-5d58-be38-48d9da4c7303" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a744da17-c832-5e19-8de7-48116074536b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38618" :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $w+x-z,$ where $w,x,y,z$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}w+3z-x-2y \\\\ 4x-2w+y-6z \\\\ 3w+5x+10z-16y \\\\ 5y-w-3z-x\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -16 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -16 & 10 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 5 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -10 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the fourth equation, we get the false statement $0=12.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124520 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0d2c2c-d738-5ed0-a0c9-d24ce63ba2d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348531} {:answer/id #uuid "40be6b67-cb1c-576f-b4e7-0c8fb4eb76eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349034} {:answer/id #uuid "543ff87a-63cd-5ecd-ae96-73b1cea7d6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349216} {:answer/id #uuid "838a9c82-e669-5eac-993b-23cb3a04318e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349650} {:answer/id #uuid "c0de0eef-d067-5d3e-81af-6995fe473535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "543ff87a-63cd-5ecd-ae96-73b1cea7d6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349216} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cd4d13f-25fc-5880-9569-aff83003f8d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "606adfb4-3c1e-53c0-a6b7-e2a4db64c71e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81237" :question/problem "If $T=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 9 & -8 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 9 & -8 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot column, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$"}]}
